2026-09-25T06:29:12.1744931Z       "body": "Discussed in https://github.com/fastapi/fastapi/discussions/16382.\n\nOn a streaming (generator) endpoint, `response_model` is only turned into a per-item field when it is left to the return annotation. Passing it explicitly silently disables both validation and the `response_model_*` options, and leaves the OpenAPI `itemSchema` empty:\n\n```python\nclass Item(BaseModel):\n    name: str\n    secret: str\n\n@app.get(\"/stream\", response_model=Item, response_model_exclude={\"secret\"})\ndef stream():\n    yield Item(name=\"public\", secret=\"private\")\n```\n\nBefore this change the endpoint streams `{\"name\": \"public\", \"secret\": \"private\"}`, so a field the developer asked to exclude is served to the client, and the OpenAPI response is `{\"application/jsonl\": {\"itemSchema\": {}}}`. Writing `response_model=Iterable[Item]` instead does not help: it is rejected outright as an invalid response field.\n\n`_populate_api_route_state` resolves the stream item type only inside the `isinstance(response_model, DefaultPlaceholder)` branch. With an explicit model, `route.stream_item_type` stays `None`, `stream_item_field` is never built, and `_serialize_data` falls back to `jsonable_encoder` plus `json.dumps`, which is the path that skips validation and the exclusion options.\n\nAn explicit `response_model` on a streaming endpoint now feeds `stream_item_field` the same way the return annotation does, and both the item type and an iterable of it are accepted. Afterwards the endpoint above streams `{\"name\": \"public\"}` and its OpenAPI response carries `itemSchema: {\"$ref\": \"#/components/schemas/Item\"}`.\n\nThe new branch only applies to generator endpoints whose response class is the default placeholder (JSONL) or `EventSourceResponse` (SSE), and it keeps the existing `ServerSentEvent` exclusion, since that is a transport wrapper rather than a data model. Non-streaming endpoints, bare iterables and return-annotation streams are untouched.\n\n## Tests\n\n`tests/test_stream_explicit_response_model.py` covers sync and async generators, `Iterable[Item]` and `AsyncIterable[Item]`, `response_model_exclude` and `response_model_exclude_none`, SSE, per-item validation, and the OpenAPI schema, together with a non-streaming endpoint to pin that its behaviour is unchanged. On `master` the module fails at import, because `response_model=Iterable[Item]` raises `FastAPIError`.\n\nThe rest of the suite passes: 2046 passed, 12 skipped (`tests/`, excluding `benchmarks` and `test_tutorial`, which need optional extras not installed here).",
